{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Let me turn aside, pray, and see.</b> The meaning of this 'turning aside' is to distance himself from that place, for as long as he distances himself from that place all the more, the eye's power over it prevails. Go and learn from the light of the sun: as long as a person draws near to that light he cannot gaze at it, but when the sun is in the east or in the west everyone gazes at it because of the great distance. So too this light that was in the bush — because of the greatness of its light Moses could not gaze at it and contemplate its essence. And so Jeremiah said, ״מֵרָחוֹק ה׳ נִרְאָה לִי״ (Jeremiah 31:2) — meaning, 'from afar,' but not from close by. And so we explained above in the perasha of וַיֵּרָא on the verse ״וַיַּרְא אֶת הַמָּקוֹם מֵרָחֹק״ (Genesis 22:4). Therefore he said 'let me turn aside, pray' — from here, to distance himself from that place, for then 'I will see' it — 'why the bush is not consumed.'" "And regarding what is written, ״וְהִנֵּה הַסְּנֶה בֹּעֵר״ — how could he say ״מַדּוּעַ לֹא יִבְעַר״? He should have said 'Why is it not consumed'! Some say that it was burning on its own, but 'why does it not burn' also to the other parts of the bush surrounding it. And some say that he judged with his intellect that it was not burning, for if it were burning why is it not consumed; therefore he said, ״מַדּוּעַ לֹא יִבְעַר״. And one may explain further: from the statement ״וַיַּרְא ה׳ כִּי סָר לִרְאוֹת״ (Exodus 3:4), hear from this that he had already turned aside from his place to draw near — or to distance himself, according to what I explained in the expression ״אָסֻרָה נָּא״. And if so, there is no place for this difficulty, for at first he thought that the bush was literally burning, and when he turned aside to see, he saw somewhat better — saw more than what he had seen before he turned aside to see — and he then saw that it was not burning at all; therefore he said, ״מַדּוּעַ לֹא יִבְעַר״. ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ אֵלָיו אַל תִּקְרַב הֲלֹם״ (Exodus 3:5) — that is to say, this distancing is a drawing-near for seeing. Know that this is so, for if the primary purpose of the prohibition had been that he not draw near to that holy place, then it should have said ״וַיַּרְא ה׳ כִּי סָר לָבוֹא״ — what is the meaning of ״כִּי סָר לִרְאוֹת״? Rather, HaShem saw that he turned aside from his place in order to see; therefore HaShem prevented him from gazing at the Elohim; therefore it says ״אַל תִּקְרַב הֲלֹם״ — a drawing-near of the eye's sight. And what He said, ״שַׁל נְעָלֶיךָ״, is not dependent on this drawing-near, but is a matter in its own right: HaShem told him to remove his shoes in the place where he was, for all of it is holy ground, for Mount Horev and all its surroundings are holy to HaShem. And this will be explained further below by way of intellect according to the approach that the commentators followed."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:09.251874105Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}